    "evidence_text": "the tokens in the source sequence  .\n\nLong documents typically cover multiple topics.\n\nas in discourse parsing  .\n\nThe two datasets annotated with extractive labels will be made public.\n\nBenefiting from the success of neural sequence\n\nto extractive summarization.\n\nThe intuition behind the method is that each hidden vector INLINEFORM3 can\n\ncapture useful information before and including the word INLINEFORM4 .\n\nthe memory of previous information.\n\nIn this way, we can represent the contextual information within each topic segment for all the sentences in that segment.\n\nWe consider two ways in which these three factors can be combined.\n\nExperiments\n\nFor completeness, we also compare with recent neural abstractive approaches.\n\nBaseline: Similar to our model, but without local context and\n\ncombination of local and global contextual information does help to identify the most important sentences (more on this in the next section).\n\nRather surprisingly, this is not the case for the global context.\n\nAdding a representation of the whole document (i.e. global context)\n\nnever significantly improves performance.\n\nFurther investigation of this finding is left as future work.\n\n[1] Rather surprisingly, it appears that the benefits of our model come only from modeling the local context.",
